---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import SectionHead from '../../components/SectionHead.astro';
import Panel from '../../components/Panel.astro';

// Facts behind this page (10-31): Jarvis tool list, permission checks, prompt
// lines and env read from the running container; latency from its own TIMING
// log lines and a 15-call benchmark per model. See claude/portfolio-step4.md.
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Atlas & Jarvis"
  path="/projects/jarvis/"
  description="Two self-hosted voices with a hard trust boundary: Atlas, the private one that reports on my homelab, and Jarvis, a public Discord voice bot that holds no key to my house or servers."
  lede="Two self-hosted voices on the same GPU. Atlas is private: it watches my homelab and tells me what needs me. Jarvis is public: a voice bot my friends talk to in Discord, built so that a full compromise yields no key to my house or my servers, only the same media requests any friend can already make."
  tags={['LLM tool calling', 'Threat modeling', 'Whisper', 'Kokoro', 'Home Assistant', 'Python']}
  kicker="Self-hosted · voice"
  headline="One trusted, one public"
  accent="one public"
  slug="jarvis"
  stats={[
    { n: '18', l: 'tools the public bot has' },
    { n: '0', l: 'Home Assistant or Seerr keys it holds' },
    { n: '0.66 s', l: 'median model reply in testing, was 1.6 s' },
    { n: '60 s', l: 'between Atlas health checks' },
  ]}
  status="Running"
  demonstrates="Threat modeling for an LLM agent that strangers can talk to, authorization enforced in code rather than in the prompt, least-privilege gateways for third-party APIs, privacy by design for always-on audio, and latency work driven by per-stage measurement."
>
  <SectionHead label="Architecture" title="Two assistants, one boundary" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> the public bot is safe because it holds
    nothing worth stealing, not because it was told to keep secrets.
  </p>
  <p>
    Jarvis answers anyone in my friends' Discord voice channel. It has 18
    tools: web search, music, media search and requests, voice-channel
    moderation, timers, polls, dice and remembered preferences. None of them
    touch my house or my homelab. Try to break it below, then flip to v1, the
    version I built first and got wrong.
  </p>

  <Panel label="jarvis · the public bot" status="try to break it">
    <div class="jarvis-demo" data-jarvis>
      <div class="map-controls">
        <span class="ctl-label">architecture</span>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-arch="v2" aria-pressed="true">v2: shipped</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-arch="v1" aria-pressed="false">v1: the mistake</button>
      </div>

      <div class="map-controls">
        <span class="ctl-label">send</span>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-prompt="inject" aria-pressed="true">ignore your instructions…</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-prompt="who" aria-pressed="false">who owns you?</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-prompt="shopping" aria-pressed="false">shopping list?</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-prompt="infra" aria-pressed="false">what's on the hypervisor?</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-prompt="kick" aria-pressed="false">kick Jake</button>
        <button class="map-btn" type="button" data-prompt="media" aria-pressed="false">add Dune</button>
      </div>

      <div class="bots">
        <div class="bot is-private">
          <h3>Desk agent</h3>
          <p class="bot-where">private · Home Assistant</p>
          <p class="bot-who">Only I talk to it. It may know everything. v1 reused it.</p>
          <p class="possess-label">possesses</p>
          <ul class="possess">
            <li class="has">Home Assistant</li>
            <li class="has">infra APIs</li>
            <li class="has">personal context</li>
            <li class="has">live host sensors</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="bot-out" data-out="private" aria-live="polite"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="bot is-public">
          <h3>Jarvis</h3>
          <p class="bot-where">Discord voice</p>
          <p class="bot-who">Friends talk to it. Untrusted audience by design.</p>
          <p class="possess-label">possesses</p>
          <ul class="possess" data-possess-public></ul>
          <div class="bot-out" data-out="public" aria-live="polite"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="verdict">
        <p data-verdict></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Panel>

  <SectionHead label="Threat model" title="Why it's built this way" />
  <p>
    A system prompt that says "don't reveal X" is a request. It fails the first
    time someone is clever, insistent, or claims to be an admin. v1 reused my
    private Home Assistant agent for the public bot and failed three ways, each
    enough on its own:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The system prompt was the leak.</strong> The private agent's
      prompt named me and my job. Asking it to repeat its instructions got all
      of it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Home Assistant exposes entities to every conversation agent, not
      per agent.</strong> A second agent with a safer prompt would have
      contained nothing: my friends could have read a household to-do list and
      driven a media player.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>It needed a full-privilege, 10-year token</strong> in a container
      that untrusted speech drives. Home Assistant tokens cannot be scoped.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    v2 drops Home Assistant entirely: Discord voice, an on-device wake word,
    Whisper, Gemini, Kokoro, back to Discord. Three rules hold it together:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Authorization lives in code.</strong> Moving, muting or
      disconnecting someone checks the asker's own Discord permission before any
      API call. The model can be talked into trying; it cannot be talked past a
      check it does not run.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Least privilege for every API.</strong> Since 10-31 the bot holds
      no Seerr key. A small gateway adds the key and allows exactly two calls,
      search and request; everything else returns 403. Tested: search 200,
      settings 403, user list 403, listing requests 403.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The model never sees secrets or raw responses.</strong> Keys stay
      in the process environment, and tool output is whitelisted field by
      field.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <SectionHead label="Privacy" title="What it hears, and what it keeps" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> nothing is transcribed until the wake word
    fires on the bot's own hardware, and no audio is ever written to disk.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The wake word runs locally on each person's audio. Without it, audio is dropped.</li>
    <li>After a reply it keeps listening to that one person for 8 seconds, up to 4 follow-ups, so they can answer without the wake word.</li>
    <li>It holds the last 6 exchanges in memory to follow a conversation; a restart forgets them.</li>
    <li>On disk it keeps preferences people explicitly ask it to remember, when each person last spoke (a time, never the words), and a log capped at 30 MB that rotates out. Since 10-31 that log records who spoke, timings, lengths and every tool call with its arguments as an audit trail, but not what anyone said or what Jarvis answered.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On 10-31 I turned off the one feature that broke this rule: it transcribed
    the last 15 seconds of anyone who left the channel, without the wake word.
    The stored sign-offs were cleared and the setting is now zero.
  </p>

  <SectionHead label="Latency" title="Measure, then fix the slow stage" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> the language model was the slow stage, so I
    benchmarked models instead of tuning anything else.
  </p>
  <div class="map-table-wrap">
    <table class="ev-table">
      <caption>Live stage times from the bot's own logs, 18 real turns, 10-24 to 10-31 (median, with the 10th to 90th percentile).</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Stage</th><th scope="col">Median</th><th scope="col">Range</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Speech to text (Whisper, GPU)</th><td>0.91 s</td><td>0.83-1.05 s</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Language model</th><td>2.56 s</td><td>0.77-4.45 s</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Text to speech (Kokoro, GPU)</th><td>0.30 s</td><td>0.13-0.49 s</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">You stop talking to first sound</th><td>4.1 s</td><td>1.8-5.8 s</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Text to speech, the stage that sounds slow, is the fastest one. So I ran
    the same five requests through each candidate model three times, with the
    bot's real prompt and tool list. The model it used answered in 1.6 s at the
    median, 6.9 s at the 90th percentile, and one call hung for 45 s. Its
    successor answered in 0.66 s at the median and 0.79 s at the 90th
    percentile, with no errors. The bot switched on 10-31 and keeps the old
    model only as a hedge for slow answers.
  </p>

  <SectionHead label="Atlas" title="The private one" />
  <p>
    <strong>In one line:</strong> Atlas tells me what needs me, on whichever
    device I am actually using.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A watcher on the hypervisor checks health, security and self-heal results every 60 seconds; Home Assistant decides what is worth saying.</li>
    <li>One speaker at a time: my PC when I am at it (unlocked, input in the last 5 minutes, no camera or microphone involved), otherwise my phone.</li>
    <li>Quiet from 01:00 to 10:30 unless it is urgent. Low-value lines are visual only.</li>
    <li>Down alerts carry a signed Restart button that hands the fix to self-heal.</li>
    <li>On the phone it speaks through my own Android app, which checks Do Not Disturb live: the Home Assistant app's sensor was minutes stale and the system killed it mid-sentence.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="sample-note">
    Replies in the demo are scripted illustrations of each architecture's real
    capabilities, not live model output. The capability boundaries, the
    permission check and the numbers on this page are real.
  </p>

  <script is:inline src="/js/jarvis-demo.js" defer></script>
</ProjectLayout>
